    {
      "ordinal": 3,
      "depth": 0,
      "kind": "section",
      "number": "2",
      "title": "Definition",
      "body": "“Tabard” is a generic term used to describe the cloth worn upon the armor. It is made to show the heraldry of the wearer and prove your wealth and status on the battlefield, or in the list.\nNowadays, Tabards have the same function as a sports jersey: to indicate the team you belong to.\n\nType of Tabards:\n\n● Sleeveless Tabard Tabards without sleeves. Simple design, common in the 13th century and 14th century. Tend to be rare in the 15th century.\n\n● Short-sleeves Tabard Tabards with short sleeves that can go up to the elbow. Common design in the 14th century.\nTend to be rare in the 15th century.\n\n● Long-sleeves Tabard Tabards with long sleeves that cover the whole arms, from shoulder to hand. Common design in the 14th and the 15th century.\n\n● Eastern Tabard We do not have many historical references for eastern Tabard. It is allowed to use the same Tabard as European armors. Eastern stylization of Tabard are displayed in the document “Eastern Armors”.\n\n● Exception for full plates armors Wearing full plates armor makes the use of Tabard not practical. Therefore, it is allowed to wear only a scarf. Scarves must follow the General Requirements (see section 3 below).\n\nExample of scarf:\n\nPicture from The Medieval Things ©",
      "page_from": 3,
      "page_to": 3,
